{
  "name": "regex-ascii-word-boundary",
  "description": "Word edges are ASCII, as in Dart's RegExp (ECMAScript): `\\b` and `\\B` count only A-Z, a-z, 0-9 and `_` as word characters, so the accented e (U+00E9) is not one. With \"|\" as the replacement, `\\b` on \"caf\u00e9 x\" gives \"|caf|\u00e9 |x|\" and `\\B` on \"caf\u00e9\" gives \"c|a|f\u00e9|\", identical under the flags `g`, `gi` and `gu` (neither `i` nor `u` alone changes the word characters). Only `i` and `u` together widen them, through Unicode case folding: the long s (U+017F) folds to s and the Kelvin sign (U+212A) to k, so `\\b` on \"\u017f\u212a\" gives \"|\u017f\u212a|\" with `giu` and finds no edge with `g`. Inside a character class `[\\b]` is the backspace U+0008, not a word edge: \"a\u0008b\" gives \"a-b\". The `matches` BoolExpr op compiles its regex with no flags: `\\bcaf\\b` matches \"caf\u00e9\" because a word edge falls between f and U+00E9. Every input reads page state, so the engine keeps the Values unresolved with `watches` and the client resolves them.",
